## Memory (callmem)

This project uses callmem for persistent memory via MCP tools. The same
`.callmem/memory.db` is shared by both OpenCode and Claude Code sessions.

**Start of session:**
1. Run `callmem briefing` (or call `mem_get_briefing` via MCP) for a live
   briefing with recent context, key entities, and open tasks.
2. Call `mem_session_start` to register this session.
3. Present a brief summary: greet the user, mention recent activity,
   highlight open TODOs, and ask what they'd like to work on.

**During the session:**
- When you make a design decision, call `mem_ingest` with type `"decision"`.
- When you identify a TODO, call `mem_ingest` with type `"todo"`.
- When you discover something notable, call `mem_ingest` with type
  `"discovery"`.
- When something fails unexpectedly, call `mem_ingest` with type `"failure"`.
- When you finish a TODO or fix a failure, call `mem_resolve` with its ID
  (accepts full or short IDs). This is the closing verb — it sets status to
  `done`/`resolved`/`cancelled`, not `mem_mark_stale`, which only hides an
  entity without recording that it was completed.
- When an entity was wrongly closed, call `mem_reopen` with its ID — the
  symmetric inverse of `mem_resolve`. It restores the correct open status
  and clears `resolved_at`; never hand-edit status via raw SQL.
- To recall past context, call `mem_search` with keywords.
- To see open tasks, call `mem_get_tasks`.
- **Before re-reading a file you've worked on before**, call
  `mem_file_context path=<file>`. If the returned timeline covers what you
  need, skip the raw read (saves ~95% of tokens).

**Long sessions (50+ messages):**
- Every ~30 messages, call `mem_check_context message_count=<n>`.
- If it returns `compress_recommended`, summarize the oldest ~30 messages
  (preserve decisions, TODOs, and failures verbatim) and call
  `mem_compress_context summary=<text>`.
- Replace the compressed span with the returned marker; recall specifics later
  via `mem_search`.

**End of session:**
- Call `mem_session_end` to trigger summary generation.

**Citing memory:**
- When a memory entity informs your work, cite its 8-character short ID
  (e.g. `#F5AVDQ25`) from the briefing. This lets readers pull up the full
  context and is the only countable proof that memory was used.

## Startup briefing

At the **start of every session**, run `callmem briefing` (or call
`mem_get_briefing` via MCP). It returns a live briefing with recent context,
key entities, and open tasks — generated fresh from `.callmem/memory.db` on
each invocation.

After reading it, present a brief summary to the user: greet them, state the
project name, mention the most recent session activity, highlight any open
TODOs or unresolved failures, and ask what they'd like to work on.
